#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921" :question/problem "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6faec47-b0e1-53cc-a122-ba924137a87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,8,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309472} {:answer/id #uuid "239ff84d-7ca6-561a-8043-85e62e7c8a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309473} {:answer/id #uuid "79bb4a2d-dafd-5be4-af5a-4aa992b2f677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309474} {:answer/id #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} {:answer/id #uuid "f344b47d-af36-5ae8-8c3d-e30f4f54e1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987c5ac6-e52d-5391-9b0f-a25f3e33e71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309475} :answer-field/id #uuid "0daf3085-8d89-5e14-92e1-e117adc2c4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f626f64-b605-58e9-8e55-f56146c3feb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129170" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e009f408-0948-583c-9f36-aa61e528c12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨6,3,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309479} {:answer/id #uuid "d5418a96-3eea-52a4-99cc-cba6ec5608b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨3,9,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309480} {:answer/id #uuid "99a54632-3487-5f66-b6f7-4f9fe3d532de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨9,6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309481} {:answer/id #uuid "154c79d3-6bc5-5eb8-9dc0-6d20092039a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨6,6,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309482} {:answer/id #uuid "cf84f720-68bf-5be5-8c1f-4f6989161d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf84f720-68bf-5be5-8c1f-4f6989161d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a533e508-2e7f-507e-9ebe-7c8ad24094f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b183e73c-612e-556a-a6fa-cdffff7f175f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129202" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $3x-2y-18=0$ and $2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b41fa7c-383b-551d-8e7d-195af093e233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309486} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef7e40-b70b-5a85-8a24-375e22f4f302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309487} {:answer/id #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} {:answer/id #uuid "2f47da4a-b316-542d-9add-3c553c97e434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309489} {:answer/id #uuid "eca8e996-c8fa-5d8f-b034-7e46ddd5ba4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6700ede1-a36b-5eee-942e-0d9f0f54f842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309488} :answer-field/id #uuid "8149ef53-3f86-5b48-a7e1-8fa0afbc8615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "923148c0-0ecb-5f91-a025-b18771e50157" :question/math-academy-id "q-129224" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d92abd1-6987-59aa-b974-08ee8d41ee28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309493} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d6e9e6-5aba-52d3-8c81-15583f3aeb82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309494} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5c003f-4cef-5c77-8d0f-1488927948d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309495} {:answer/id #uuid "855cd76b-6735-5058-9299-202e4e034510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309496} {:answer/id #uuid "a839178d-05a9-56fa-90eb-8225e7dba44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855cd76b-6735-5058-9299-202e4e034510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309496} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a6fea0-fd9c-5954-a8d9-c8f50d9254ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8065d082-8b78-5fe6-a484-46d6c36cc283" :question/math-academy-id "q-129239" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-5y+z+1=0$ and $3x-z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bc9956-3f2a-5751-9955-380cc068e4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb4e7da-d423-537d-b6d1-eca94ff0ffac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309501} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd6c990-9990-5e92-9979-5092fd03374b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309502} {:answer/id #uuid "6a28c4a1-3a4b-5b47-8e2a-096224035b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309503} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbba08a-aee5-5bc2-b685-3b92ad7ff18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbba08a-aee5-5bc2-b685-3b92ad7ff18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309504} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6f1dbf-64e4-5fbc-ab2f-a0d761ede179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0531b19e-756e-5c97-abd0-1f5e648bd0cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129259" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939e9973-cb8d-54aa-a7e6-965adb9bf94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146876} {:answer/id #uuid "b091c44e-c006-5b37-85b6-190cdc188a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146877} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fe2a3a-8c62-5389-a161-a25f6e8a33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146878} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2bcc97-cf0e-5657-8010-ae978b880459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146879} {:answer/id #uuid "68a96f68-b656-59fb-9345-0525fd9ebd33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "939e9973-cb8d-54aa-a7e6-965adb9bf94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146876} :answer-field/id #uuid "57427c13-e607-5012-b842-cb76e784fdd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddee53f3-5b0d-5dc0-9b9e-825db292f3e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71110" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98dad773-e343-5720-b6d0-06acff1fcc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-1, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146894} {:answer/id #uuid "2df7b237-0cf7-5db4-a613-d9911a551947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-1, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146895} {:answer/id #uuid "37ff0265-8d39-5cfc-b9a1-4b42b3ef6549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-5, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146896} {:answer/id #uuid "4b250100-69cb-525f-8e37-ceb2e6d50081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146897} {:answer/id #uuid "669966ee-f3cb-5ad2-81fa-197cc11629e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b250100-69cb-525f-8e37-ceb2e6d50081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146897} :answer-field/id #uuid "50693ecf-036a-5c40-a007-20ea1b1a1626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c01a5d8f-f2ce-5a5d-bfd0-e648dd7fcc1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71187" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79364c79-ba80-5f61-a84c-9ccc26656702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309507} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2f075d-7b05-5e6a-985a-6ddffd332a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309508} {:answer/id #uuid "588c1bd1-72fd-5ee8-b43b-803755836475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309509} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbb16e7-a03e-5861-ac67-26d1fa111640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309510} {:answer/id #uuid "bf46c8a5-33f3-526a-9f51-cc90d167d3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79364c79-ba80-5f61-a84c-9ccc26656702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309507} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2fa7d8-c0f7-5d77-8cd7-60912214c498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c61741c-6473-5890-871e-f84802851c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-71251" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y-z+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $y-z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d83a033-b0bb-5438-9f8c-28682bfe8259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309514} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3b5358-8006-5f3d-bd4c-0610a60bd282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309515} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bba63b-484c-5630-8ed5-53736d94d609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,4,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} {:answer/id #uuid "5cda850d-37a2-5cea-8236-54a98a8db142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309517} {:answer/id #uuid "7af602aa-4c9c-5c7b-b126-9ce6cba28e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0bba63b-484c-5630-8ed5-53736d94d609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,4,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd13293d-5834-5841-8e4b-8389e288fec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98b4ea6a-7308-564a-bbb7-8da49e04b365" :question/math-academy-id "q-71260" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+3z=0 \\\\ 4y+2z-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-2x+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y+2z-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨0,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨-12,4,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b0ce8b6-6dbe-5dea-9b88-5729aa59ac33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-5,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309521} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d6712-803b-5fda-86ca-914e95f9f438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20,25,20⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309522} {:answer/id #uuid "3b15c3cb-3433-5846-8d12-1c834085ece6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309523} {:answer/id #uuid "147e4708-d07d-5614-ae6d-4ca7d55b1f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309524} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ec77c1-ae66-5a52-b343-ea088b631874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,-4,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2d6712-803b-5fda-86ca-914e95f9f438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20,25,20⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309522} :answer-field/id #uuid "887dd48c-ca9b-539c-a987-adbe261b4e19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13385c8f-4361-5ebf-99b7-4f21bd7e90b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71266" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y=0 \\\\ 4y-5z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d3777c-931e-54be-bc36-4a01094158c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309528} {:answer/id #uuid "2976cdff-3ec8-5aeb-9e6f-7febfe55f031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309529} {:answer/id #uuid "823c751c-6086-558b-a81f-7377b09e6e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309530} {:answer/id #uuid "570af63d-4fcd-572a-8beb-b6e23ef63681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309531} {:answer/id #uuid "3d216364-6877-5cf5-b473-f039b81e2723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "823c751c-6086-558b-a81f-7377b09e6e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309530} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e29a774-9bd2-54dd-8c69-555bcacd95c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce5171f6-22a8-5be0-9547-0d96404368c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71268" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $z=1$ and $x=y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $z=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=y$ (or $x-y=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca85272d-c8e6-5de8-ae5f-276509834959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309535} {:answer/id #uuid "a56f4364-17c3-5089-86db-c35c7232a8ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309536} {:answer/id #uuid "60692775-03e2-554e-a6c2-463e42103a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309537} {:answer/id #uuid "2a60b638-d8b1-57b4-b880-3bd292690ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb7bccb-6b13-538d-a7d9-6876fef93242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a60b638-d8b1-57b4-b880-3bd292690ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309538} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d55ebd-d47b-5681-8adf-7c27083248c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b23b0cec-a767-5778-8e67-5c10a6b25080" :question/math-academy-id "q-71347" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-y+6=0$ and $y+2z-2=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x-0+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2z-2=0\\,⟹\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+2z-2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i-4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3-1=-4,$ and $b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3574aa75-7514-5202-b4e9-949399ecaa1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨2,1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309542} {:answer/id #uuid "99ea64db-fa3c-5995-a6f1-668a9a79a49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309543} {:answer/id #uuid "ac61c71e-c9b1-518c-9f17-bc751449a034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨1,3,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309544} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc437d3-cdd7-512b-925b-8d2c6cd42861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨2,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309545} {:answer/id #uuid "bed335ac-6bd0-574b-bda3-8d868ba09685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed335ac-6bd0-574b-bda3-8d868ba09685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309546} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7522a2-8365-5bbe-a942-7e6cfc58e6e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65aeb577-5179-51b6-b6d7-0edba5765814" :question/math-academy-id "q-71447" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y-4z+6=0$ and $x-4y+2z=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n0-4y+2z=0\\,⟹\\,z=2y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $z=2y$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y-4z+6 & =0 \\\\ 0+2y-4(2y)+6 & =0 \\\\ -6y & =-6 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $z=2y=2$ and $a=⟨0,1,2⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-4z+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,-4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-4y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,-4⟩\\times ⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i-6j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-12,-6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{6}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨2,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,2⟩+t⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+2=3,$ and $b=⟨2,1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d675ab-e687-5379-87fd-7d6a7a9dbe34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309549} {:answer/id #uuid "3d31a61a-dbc2-505a-86ae-2a4a676ebe47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309550} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd53607-311c-576e-87f4-c588898f367d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309551} {:answer/id #uuid "f81afe72-8572-5b2e-b772-60383468c072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309552} {:answer/id #uuid "276df41e-3ae0-5ff0-a1c6-66219178b6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276df41e-3ae0-5ff0-a1c6-66219178b6a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309553} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f73c74d-d549-5390-8101-8d61fca29ba6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4895b4d9-6e99-5443-b10a-85bc7b1b4155" :question/math-academy-id "q-71456" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-3y+z=0$ and $2x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,5,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-3,1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+2j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,5⟩\\ne ⟨1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a9f90ff-04ab-56f3-a990-b883575306c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309556} {:answer/id #uuid "c446fdfd-caf2-5a5e-ab87-c16c8089c4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309557} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c67c65-8b86-54b4-97dc-1f02d1fcef4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309558} {:answer/id #uuid "13f99e1d-6f89-5603-aace-967a83531901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309559} {:answer/id #uuid "03301ccb-4a15-5dee-83e2-cf25ca472484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7c67c65-8b86-54b4-97dc-1f02d1fcef4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309558} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3652218-a27d-5c9d-9f9c-1185dc023fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a246de7e-bc39-5688-8da7-0fdc8121bc01" :question/math-academy-id "q-71468" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-4y+6z+2=0$ and $-x+2y-3z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a straight line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-4y+6z+2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-4,6⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x+2y-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-4,6⟩\\times ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i+0j+0k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}=0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes are also parallel. So, the planes can't intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed,  the second plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the first plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n2(0)-4(0)+6(0)+2=2\\ne 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes can't be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}]}
